  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...Inverted trough will continue to push west \nacross the area this afternoon into tonight. Scattered to numerous \nshowers and storms, mainly across the interior this afternoon will \nagain shift west of the area this evening. Stronger storm potential \nremains low, as there remains limited drier air aloft due to the \nvery moist airmass in place and also warm temps exist aloft (-5C at \n500mb). The main concern will be the potential for locally heavy \nrainfall, with localized totals of 2-4\" possible from any slow \nmoving or persistent storms. This may lead to mostly minor flooding \nissues of roadways and poor drainage areas. A Marginal Risk for \nexcessive rainfall remains in place across the area today. There \nwill likely remain a few lingering showers and possibly a storm or \ntwo past sunset. As the inverted trough shifts west, low level flow \nwill remain onshore out of the E/SE, which combined with deep \nmoisture in place will continue the potential for scattered showers \nand isolated storms that can push onshore along the coast overnight. \n\nThursday-Friday...Ridge axis of high pressure over the west Atlantic \nremains near to north of the area through late week. Surface winds \nwill generally remain onshore through the period, out of the E/SE \nThursday and SE on Friday. Deep moisture is reinforced across the \narea, with anomalously high PW values of 2.2-2.4\", and a mid-\nlevel disturbance moving in from the east will continue to keep \nrain chances above normal each day. The trend in early start to \nshowers and storms will continue, with showers and isolated storms\nbeginning to form from mid to late morning, with coverage of this\nactivity becoming scattered to numerous into the afternoon. DCAPE\nvalues remain low due to the saturated airmass and temps aloft \nremain warm (-4 to -5C at 500mb), so strong storm potential will \ncontinue to be low. Will see occasional lightning strikes from \nstorms, and potentially gusty winds up to 30-40 mph. Main threat \nfrom storms will continue to be locally heavy rainfall of 2-4\" and\nminor flooding from slow moving storms. There is a very low (up \nto 5%) chance for isolated spots to receive around 5\" of rainfall \nleading to greater flooding impacts, and the Weather Prediction \nCenter has outlooked all of East Central Florida in a Marginal \n(level 1 of 4) risk for excessive rainfall each day.\n\nHigher rain chances and increased afternoon cloud cover will keep \nhighs in the upper 80s and low 90s. Peak heat index values up to 100-\n107F will occur ahead of showers and storms. This will still lead to \na Moderate to locally Major HeatRisk, so be sure to take frequent \nbreaks in the shade or air conditioning if spending prolonged time \noutdoors to avoid the development of heat-related illness. \n\nSaturday-Tuesday...(Previous Discussion) Upper-level pattern \nstagnates for a few days as the troughs/lows over the west and east \ncoasts become semi-stationary, keeping deep ridging over the Central \nUS. The prominent low pressure system off the New England \ncoast/Canadian Maritimes will quickly push the ridge axis of the \nBermuda well south of Florida, and the local pressure gradient \nbecomes almost non-existent. The official forecast calls for \noffshore flow to develop Saturday before becoming light Sunday \nonward, but it's very possible Saturday may end up being \nlight/variable was well. A front associated with the aforementioned \nlow arrives in the Deep South this weekend, but how far it gets and \nhow the boundary evolves Monday onward is highly uncertain. Models \ndo agree the high moisture isn't going anywhere unless the front \nreaches Central Florida, and broad above normal rain chances \ncontinue through the period. Seasonable temperatures will be on the \nhotter side inland Saturday and Sunday, then are forecast to return \nback to normal for the work week.",
